> I'm not sure I understand what you mean here.

Well, roughly speaking, a vrfy handler with a structure like https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/master/kernel/msg_q.c#L92 . Suppose we'd want to validate something *inside* struct k_msgq *q. Per TOCTOU rules, reliable way to do that would be to make a copy first:

~~~
struct k_msgq s = *q;

validate(s.some_field);
impl(&s);
~~~

My concern is that oversmart modern compilers could optimize that to:

~~~
validate(q->some_field);
impl(q);
~~~

To guaranteedly preclude that, we'd need to do `volatile struct k_msgq s = *q;`. And I don't believe I ever saw `volatile` keyword in any vrfy function, hence my question.
